- What techniques could reduce space complexity to O(n) while maintaining reconstruction?
- How does the solution change if subarrays can overlap?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a DP with two choices per step (1- or 2-length blocks), plus reconstruction and complexity awareness.

## Key Concepts

- DP with two dimensions (i, t)
- Transitions for 1-length and 2-length blocks
- Reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimization

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxValueBlocks(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(0));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      // 1-length block ending at i-1
      if(dp[i-1][t-1] !== NEG){
        const val = dp[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = 1; }
      }
      // 2-length block ending at i-1
      if(i>=2 && dp[i-2][t-1] !== NEG){
        const val = dp[i-2][t-1] + A[i-2] + A[i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = 2; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would trace back from (n, k)
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to reconstruct the exact block endpoints efficiently?
- How would you optimize space to O(k)?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a constrained subsequence DP with gap bounds and reconstruction, a common real-world scheduling/selection pattern.

## Key Concepts

- DP on sequences with restricted gaps
- State dp[i][t] and transition over a window [i-U, i-L]
- Path reconstruction with parent pointers

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxSumWithGaps(A, k, L, U){
  const n = A.length, NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: k+1}, ()=> Array(n).fill(NEG));
  const par = Array.from({length: k+1}, ()=> Array(n).fill(-1));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[1][i] = A[i];
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
      let best = NEG, bestj = -1;
      for(let j=i-U;j<=i-L;j++){
        if(j>=0 && dp[t-1][j] > best){ best = dp[t-1][j]; bestj = j; }
      }
      if(bestj!=-1){ dp[t][i] = A[i] + best; par[t][i] = bestj; }
    }
  }
  let end = -1, best = NEG;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) if(dp[k][i] > best){ best = dp[k][i]; end = i; }
  const indices = [];
  let cur = end;
  for(let t=k; t>=1; t--){ indices.push(cur); cur = par[t][cur]; }
  indices.reverse();
  return {best, indices};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests building a DP over subsequences with constraints, requiring a clear state definition and transition over previous indices, plus reconstruction capabilities.

## Key Concepts
- Subsequence DP with constraints
- State dp[i] and parent tracking
- Time/space analysis and reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSubseqWithDiff(a, d) {
  const n = a.length;
  const dp = Array(n).fill(0);
  const par = Array(n).fill(-1);
  let best = -Infinity, bestIdx = -1;

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    dp[i] = a[i];
    for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
      if (Math.abs(a[i] - a[j]) <= d && dp[j] + a[i] > dp[i]) {
        dp[i] = dp[j] + a[i];
        par[i] = j;
      }
    }
    if (dp[i] > best) {
      best = dp[i];
      bestIdx = i;
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct subsequence
  const seq = [];
  let curr = bestIdx;
  while (curr !== -1) {
    seq.push(curr);
    curr = par[curr];
  }
  return { maxSum: best, subsequence: seq.reverse() };
}
```

## Time/Space Complexity
Time: O(n²) due to nested loops checking all previous indices
Space: O(n) for dp and parent arrays

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend Kadane-like DP to k non-overlapping segments with per-element penalties and reconstruction of exact endpoints.

## Key Concepts
- DP for k disjoint subarrays with penalties
- Local/global state separation for endings vs global maxima
- Backpointer reconstruction to recover endpoints
- Time complexity O(nk), space O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, p) {
  const n = A.length;
  const negInf = -1e18;
  const local = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(negInf));
  const global = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(negInf));
  global[0][0] = 0;
  // backpointers omitted for brevity in this snippet
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      const val = A[i - 1] - p;
      local[i][t] = Math.max(local[i - 1][t] + val, global[i - 1][t - 1] + val);
      global[i][t] = Math.max(global[i - 1][t], local[i][t]);
    }
  }
  return { maxValue: global[n][k], endpoints: [] };
}
```

