## Why This Is Asked

This problem evaluates the ability to design a dynamic programming solution for selecting exactly k vertex-disjoint directed paths in a DAG while maximizing total node weights. It is a common pattern in interviews at Netflix, Uber, and Meta.

## Key Concepts

- **Topological ordering** ensures DP processes vertices in valid dependency order
- **DP over path count dimension** (t from 1 to k) tracks the maximum weight for t paths ending at each vertex
- **Vertex-disjoint constraint** is naturally enforced through the state definition, since each path is built incrementally
- **Parent pointer array** enables reconstruction of the selected paths after DP computation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code for k vertex-disjoint path selection

function selectKPaths(N, M, edges, weights, k) {
  const dp = Array(N).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array(N).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  // Get topological order
  const topo = topologicalSort(N, edges);

  // Initialize sources
  for (const s of sources) {
    dp[s][1] = weights[s];
  }

  // Process in topological order
  for (const u of topo) {
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      for (let t = 1; t < k; t++) {
        if (dp[u][t] > -Infinity) {
          const newWeight = dp[u][t] + weights[v];
          if (newWeight > dp[v][t + 1]) {
            dp[v][t + 1] = newWeight;
            parent[v][t + 1] = u;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct paths using parent pointers
  return reconstructPaths(dp, parent, k);
}
```

## Why This Problem Is Asked
This classic problem evaluates your ability to design clean DP states for interval selection with reconstruction requirements. It strikes the right balance—accessible to beginners while effectively testing state structuring and backtracking skills.

## Key Concepts
- **dp[i][t]** manages selections across the first i elements with at most t subarrays
- **local[i][t]** distinguishes between extending an existing subarray and starting a new one
- Backtracking through the DP table to reconstruct segment boundaries

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumSubarrays(nums, k) {
    if (!nums.length || k <= 0) return { maxSum: 0, subarrays: [] };

    const n = nums.length;
    const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
    const local = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
    const endIdx = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));

    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(k, i); t++) {
            local[i][t] = Math.max(
                local[i - 1][t] + nums[i - 1],
                dp[i - 1][t - 1] + nums[i - 1]
            );
            if (local[i][t] === dp[i - 1][t - 1] + nums[i - 1]) {
                endIdx[i][t] = i - 1;
            }
            dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t] >= local[i][t] ? dp[i - 1][t] : local[i][t];
        }
    }

    const subarrays = [];
    let j = n, t = k;
    while (t > 0 && j > 0) {
        if (endIdx[j][t] !== -1) {
            let start = endIdx[j][t];
            while (start > 0 && endIdx[start][t] !== -1) start--;
            subarrays.unshift([start, endIdx[j][t]]);
            j = start;
            t--;
        } else {
            break;
        }
    }

    return { maxSum: dp[n][k], subarrays };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a fresh DP angle: interval-based value depends on distinct characters within each substring, plus precise reconstruction for k blocks.

## Key Concepts

- Non-overlapping interval DP
- Precomputing distinct counts for all substrings
- Reconstruction via back-pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch
function maxKSubstrings(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dist = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    const freq = new Array(26).fill(0);
    let setBits = 0;
    for (let j = i; j >= 0; j--) {
      const idx = s.charCodeAt(j) - 97;
      if (freq[idx]++ === 0) setBits++;
      dist[j][i] = setBits;
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  const start = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    dp[i][0] = 0;
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      // Option 1: skip i
      if (dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
        prev[i][t] = i-1;
        start[i][t] = start[i-1][t];
      }

      // Option 2: end t-th substring at i
      for (let s = 1; s <= i; s++) {
        const val = dp[s-1][t-1] + dist[s-1][i-1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          prev[i][t] = s-1;
          start[i][t] = s-1;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct substrings
  const result = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    if (prev[i][t] === i-1) {
      i--;
    } else {
      result.unshift([start[i][t]+1, i]);
      i = prev[i][t];
      t--;
    }
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], substrings: result };
}
```

Time complexity: O(n²k) for DP transitions + O(n²) for preprocessing. Space: O(n² + nk). Optimizations include limiting the inner loop or using convex hull tricks for special cases.

